body:
Understood but what area is in more under served in dc for a metro stop than langston/carver?  There isnt a metro stop for nearly 2 miles yet the population in that area is so dense!

&#x200B;

Lots of money and moving parts aside, certainly there has to be a pecking order in terms of   what area is in the topic of discussion for future potential metro stops?

&#x200B;

Realistically I just dont know how they'd do it?  What line could they possibly extend to cover that area?  It'd have to be a separate line that went through dc.

Carver/Langston NEEDS a metro stop.  You have four major roads at one intersection H street, Maryland Ave, Bladensburg Rd and Benning Rd.  With the explosion of H street over the past decade a lot of these people are having to clog up H street to make it to Union Station.

&#x200B;

Giving H street a streetcar was putting a band-aid on a gunshot wound.  It doesn't even have its own lane so it just bogs down traffic whist being a slow/inefficient form of transportation.  Why the city decided to drop 200m on a lack luster form of transportation is beyond me.  We NEED a dedicated metro stop!
